It’s easier to read large numerical values when they’re separated by a comma every three digits. For instance, your brain can more easily comprehend **5,000,000** at a glance than **5000000**. Polars allows you to format numerical values with commas to improve readability. Below is a dataframe showing department budgets for a company:

::: {#e91758f5 .cell execution_count=1}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```{=html}
<div><style>
.dataframe > thead > tr,
.dataframe > tbody > tr {
  text-align: right;
  white-space: pre-wrap;
}
</style>
<small>shape: (5, 2)</small><table border="1" class="dataframe"><thead><tr><th>Department</th><th>Budget</th></tr><tr><td>str</td><td>i64</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;Hospitality&quot;</td><td>5700000</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Legal&quot;</td><td>1200000</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Finance&quot;</td><td>8300000</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Advertising&quot;</td><td>6800000</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Sales&quot;</td><td>9200000</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
```
:::
:::


<br> 

## Insert commas in values

We can make the budget values more human-readable by separating each group of three digits with a comma. Here’s how to do it:

::: {#b0e3cd8c .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
with pl.Config(thousands_separator=','):
    display(df)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
```{=html}
<div><style>
.dataframe > thead > tr,
.dataframe > tbody > tr {
  text-align: right;
  white-space: pre-wrap;
}
</style>
<small>shape: (5, 2)</small><table border="1" class="dataframe"><thead><tr><th>Department</th><th>Budget</th></tr><tr><td>str</td><td>i64</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;Hospitality&quot;</td><td>5,700,000</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Legal&quot;</td><td>1,200,000</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Finance&quot;</td><td>8,300,000</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Advertising&quot;</td><td>6,800,000</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Sales&quot;</td><td>9,200,000</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
```
:::
:::


<br>

If you live in Brazil (where the thousand separator is a full stop), you can replace `,` with `.`

::: {#a4db00c8 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
with pl.Config(thousands_separator='.'):
    display(df)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
